WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.670
Western North America's history begins with late Precambrian rifting, broadly around

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:07.290
six hundred million years ago.

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:10.430
The identity of the departing landmass remains uncertain.

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:14.450
By early Paleozoic time, the thinned western edge was a passive

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:16.430
margin with a broad shallow shelf.

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:21.510
Shallow-water deposits accumulated near the continent, with deeper-water sediment farther west.

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:24.670
The coastline and the plate boundary were no longer the same

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:24.970
thing.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:29.460
During the middle and late Paleozoic, island arcs developed offshore and

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:30.900
some collided with the margin.

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:35.940
Thrusting moved deeper-water rocks eastward over shallower-water deposits and created a

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:40.940
foreland basin. Meanwhile, the Ancestral Rockies farther inland are interpreted here

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:43.900
as a response to collisions in the east, not simply the

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:48.000
western arc collisions. Events happening at similar times need not share

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:49.280
the nearest apparent cause.

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:55.170
Mesozoic subduction produced volcanic arcs and large intrusions, including the Sierra

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:59.470
Nevada batholith. Terranes such as Wrangellia approached the continent.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:01.890
Compression built a long thrust belt;

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:04.610
its load bent the continental interior downward.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:08.910
Combined with high sea level, that subsidence helped an interior seaway

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:10.990
spread from the Gulf region toward the Arctic.

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:16.590
Magmatism, shortening, loading, and sedimentation belonged to the same regional system.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:20.440
Around eighty million years ago, the slab began descending at a

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:23.360
shallower angle. In this model, it approached the base of the

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:28.480
overriding lithosphere, shifted magmatism inland, and increased compression far from the

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:32.280
coast. The Laramide orogeny formed uplifts and basins in the Rocky

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:35.180
Mountain region from about eighty to forty million years ago.

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:38.760
Changing the slab's geometry changed where the upper plate responded.

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:42.610
Later, convergence slowed and the stress pattern changed.

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:46.410
Extension spread through much of the Southwest, building the Basin and

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:48.230
Range and the Rio Grande Rift.

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:51.650
Parts of the margin evolved into the San Andreas transform system

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:54.150
as North America overran a spreading center.

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:58.910
The textbook describes major regional stretching, but not uniform extension everywhere.

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:04.110
Today's western landscape combines old collision structures, magmatic rocks, younger fault

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:06.030
blocks, and active plate boundaries.
